# Day 18 | How Much Life Is on Mars?

NULL · #30DayMapChallenge 2026

The map asks how many living organisms there are per square kilometre on
Mars. No observations support a density estimate anywhere on the planet.
The single legend entry applies to the entire Martian surface.

[Final map](outputs/day18-null-mars.png)

## Tools

Python, PyGMT, GMT, Ghostscript and Pillow. The bundled Roboto Regular and
Bold fonts are used throughout the layout.

## Workflow

1. Load the global Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter (MOLA) grid with
   `pygmt.datasets.load_mars_relief()`. The AGU24 workshop's section 0.1 starts
   at `01d` (1°) and plots the grid in Mollweide with `Figure.grdimage()`.
2. Keep the workshop's global relief and Mollweide workflow. Use a 15
   arc-minute (0.25°) grid in the final render to retain more relief detail.
   Centre the map on 180°E and draw all 360° of longitude and both poles.
   Use muted Martian tones and soft hillshade for orientation and omit the
   workshop's elevation colour bar.
3. Place official names from the Gazetteer's current Mars control network.
   The CSV preserves planetocentric latitude and positive-east longitude
   in the 0–360° convention. MOLA also uses degrees east. The script wraps
   the longitudes relative to the central meridian and applies the same
   spherical Mollweide projection to the label anchors.
4. Add a north arrow and a 2,000 km bar calibrated at the equator. The
   complete equatorial map width represents one circumference at the IAU
   Mars equatorial radius of 3,396.19 km.
5. Compose the print layout with the global NULL legend, source credits
   and challenge footer.

## Local data and reproduction

The [toponyms CSV](data/mars-toponyms.csv) stores the catalogue centres,
Gazetteer feature IDs and label offsets in millimetres. The first run
downloads MOLA relief into the GMT cache. Subsequent runs can reuse it.

From the project folder, run:

```sh
conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate day18-null
python day18-null.py
```

To reproduce the tutorial-resolution comparison, run
`python day18-null.py --resolution 01d`. Run the command without the flag
afterward to restore the 15 arc-minute output at the same path.

## Sources and inspiration

- **Tutorial.** GMT/PyGMT AGU24 workshop, Tutorial 5, section 0.1.
  [2-D map view](https://www.generic-mapping-tools.org/agu24workshop/tut05_topography.html#d-map-view)
- **Relief.** NASA Mars Global Surveyor, Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter.
  [USGS MOLA DEM](https://astrogeology.usgs.gov/search/map/mars_mgs_mola_dem_463m) ·
  [PyGMT MOLA grids](https://www.pygmt.org/v0.17.0/api/generated/pygmt.datasets.load_mars_relief.html)
- **Place names.** IAU/USGS Gazetteer of Planetary Nomenclature.
  [Olympus Mons](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/4453) ·
  [Elysium Mons](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/1783) ·
  [Valles Marineris](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/6288) ·
  [Hellas Planitia](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/2432) ·
  [Utopia Planitia](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/6260) ·
  [Jezero](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/14300) ·
  [Gale](https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/2071)
- **Mars radius.** IAU 2000 equatorial radius.
  [USGS projection reference](https://astrogeology.usgs.gov/docs/concepts/camera-geometry-and-projections/learning-about-map-projections/)
- **Type.** Roboto Regular and Bold are bundled with their
  [license](data/Roboto-LICENSE.txt).

## Notes

- **NULL and zero.** NULL means the density is unknown. Zero would be a
  numerical estimate of zero organisms per square kilometre. The map uses
  one global NULL status and contains no numerical organism-density layer.
- **Print at actual size.** The exported PNG is 300 × 225 mm at 300 dpi
  (3543 × 2657 px). The map keeps 10 mm side margins, and the bottom edge
  is trimmed by 5 mm from the original layout.

